For a C++ staff-level interview, the best way to compare a mutex-based counter with an atomic counter is to run the same workload with multiple threads, 
measure elapsed time using `std::chrono`, and verify that both implementations produce the correct final count.

The key difference:

- Mutex: protects the increment operation using locking and unlocking.
- Atomic: uses `std::atomic::fetch_add()` to increment safely without an explicit mutex.
- Performance: atomics often perform better for a simple counter, but contention, hardware, and memory ordering affect the result.

## 1. Complete C++ benchmark

This program compares both approaches using the same number of threads and increments.

```

#include <atomic>
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <iomanip>

using namespace std;
using namespace std::chrono;

constexpr int NUM_THREADS = 4;
constexpr int64_t INCREMENTS_PER_THREAD = 5'000'000;

// Mutex-based counter
class MutexCounter {
private:
    int64_t count = 0;
    mutex mtx;

public:
    void increment() {
        lock_guard<mutex> lock(mtx);
        ++count;
    }

    int64_t get() const {
        // Call only after all worker threads have joined.
        return count;
    }
};

// Atomic-based counter
class AtomicCounter {
private:
    atomic<int64_t> count{0};

public:
    void increment() {
        count.fetch_add(1, memory_order_relaxed);
    }

    int64_t get() const {
        return count.load(memory_order_relaxed);
    }
};

// Measure the elapsed time of a workload
template <typename Counter>
double benchmark(Counter& counter) {
    vector<thread> workers;
    workers.reserve(NUM_THREADS);

    auto start = steady_clock::now();

    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_THREADS; ++i) {
        workers.emplace_back([&counter]() {
            for (int64_t j = 0;
                 j < INCREMENTS_PER_THREAD; ++j) {
                counter.increment();
            }
        });
    }

    for (auto& worker : workers) {
        worker.join();
    }

    auto end = steady_clock::now();

    return duration<double, milli>(end - start).count();
}

int main() {
    MutexCounter mutexCounter;
    AtomicCounter atomicCounter;

    double mutexTime = benchmark(mutexCounter);
    double atomicTime = benchmark(atomicCounter);

    const int64_t expected =
        NUM_THREADS * INCREMENTS_PER_THREAD;

    cout << fixed << setprecision(2);

    cout << "Threads: " << NUM_THREADS << '\n';
    cout << "Expected count: " << expected << "\n\n";

    cout << "Mutex counter:\n";
    cout << "  Count = " << mutexCounter.get() << '\n';
    cout << "  Time  = " << mutexTime << " ms\n\n";

    cout << "Atomic counter:\n";
    cout << "  Count = " << atomicCounter.get() << '\n';
    cout << "  Time  = " << atomicTime << " ms\n\n";

    cout << "Atomic speedup: "
         << mutexTime / atomicTime << "x\n";

    return 0;
}

```

## 2. Compile and run

On Ubuntu, save the code as `counter_benchmark.cpp` and execute:

```
g++ -O2 -std=c++17 -pthread counter_benchmark.cpp -o counter_benchmark
./counter_benchmark
```

Example output (illustrative; actual results depend on your CPU):

```
Threads: 4
Expected count: 20000000

Mutex counter:
  Count = 20000000
  Time  = 4200.00 ms

Atomic counter:
  Count = 20000000
  Time  = 1100.00 ms

Atomic speedup: 3.82x
```

Both counters must produce exactly `20,000,000`. The times above are examples, not measured results from your machine.

## 3. Why use `memory_order_relaxed`?

```
count.fetch_add(1, memory_order_relaxed);
```

For a standalone counter, relaxed ordering is sufficient because we only need atomicity of the increment, not synchronization of other memory accesses through the counter.

- `memory_order_relaxed`: guarantees atomic modification of the counter without imposing additional ordering constraints.
- Default `fetch_add(1)`: uses sequential consistency (`memory_order_seq_cst`), which provides stronger ordering guarantees and can have different performance.

## 4. Important performance considerations

| Factor               | Mutex counter                | Atomic counter                               |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Thread safety        | Yes                          | Yes                                          |
| Explicit lock/unlock | Yes                          | No                                           |
| Contention           | Threads wait for the lock    | Threads contend for the same atomic variable |
| Scalability          | Lock overhead and contention | Cache-line contention can limit scalability  |
| Guaranteed lock-free | No                           | No                                           |

Interview insight: An atomic counter is not necessarily lock-free on every platform, and it does not eliminate contention. When many CPU cores update the same atomic variable, 
cache-line ownership transfers can become expensive.

For a more reliable benchmark, repeat each test several times, compare median timings, and test different thread counts. Also consider a per-thread counter followed by aggregation: 
it can outperform both approaches when the workload permits independent counters.
